      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we first note that this list can be rewritten using the transpose of the first expression. Specifically, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000n^2}{1000^2} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{10^4} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\left( \\frac{n}{10} \\right)^2 \\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nThus, the list becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000}{10} \\right\\rfloor^2.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{4}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{9}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000}{10} \\right\\rfloor^2 = 0, 0, 0, \\dots, 0, 1, 1, \\dots, 9, 9, \\dots, 10^2.\n\\]\n\nWe now need to determine the distinct outputs of the function \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). The smallest value is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\) and the largest value is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\). We need to check if all integers from 0 to 1000 are included in this range and thus are attained as values of the function.\n\nLet's verify if we can achieve all values from 0 to 1000. The value \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) must be an integer between 0 and 1000. For any integer \\(k\\) in this range, there exists an \\(n\\) such that:\n\n\\[\nk = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\implies k \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < k+1 \\implies 1000k \\leq n^2 < 1000(k+1) \\implies \\sqrt{1000k} \\leq n < \\sqrt{1000(k+1)}.\n\\]\n\nThis means that for any \\(k\\) from 0 to 1000, there is an integer \\(n\\) such that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = k\\). Therefore, all integers from 0 to 1000 are distinct values of the function.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1001}.\n\\]",
