      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The expression \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) gives the greatest integer less than or equal to \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\). We need to find the distinct values of \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( n \\) ranging from 1 to 1000.\n\nFirst, let's determine the range of \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\):\n- When \\( n = 1 \\), \\( \\frac{1^2}{1000} = \\frac{1}{1000} \\), so \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\).\n- When \\( n = 1000 \\), \\( \\frac{1000^2}{1000} = 1000 \\), so \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000 \\).\n\nFor \\( n \\) ranging from 1 to 1000, \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\) ranges from \\( \\frac{1}{1000} \\) to 1000. The floor function \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) will take on every integer value from 0 to 1000, inclusive.\n\nThus, the number of distinct values of \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) is 1001 (from 0 to 1000 inclusive).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{1001}\\).\n\nHere's the Python code to verify this reasoning:\n\n```python\n# Let's calculate the distinct values of floor(n^2 / 1000) for n = 1 to 1000\ndistinct_values = set()\n\nfor n in range(1, 1001):\n    value = (n**2) // 1000\n    distinct_values.add(value)\n\n# The number of distinct values\nnum_distinct_values = len(distinct_values)\nprint(num_distinct_values)\n```",
